        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long will a portable power station power a house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It will not power a whole house in the usual sense. A 2,000Wh unit can keep a refrigerator, lights, phones and internet running for roughly a day, depending on the fridge and how much else is plugged in. Running heating, cooling or cooking appliances drains it in hours or less."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a power station better than a generator for emergencies?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For short outages and indoor use, yes: it is silent, needs no fuel and is safe indoors. For long outages or heavy loads, a generator delivers far more energy per dollar. Many households keep both, using the generator during the day and the power station overnight. Safety and convenience favor the battery for most people."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are cheap power stations worth buying?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Small, inexpensive units are fine for phones, lights and a CPAP. Be cautious with bargain units from unknown brands: check for LiFePO4 chemistry, a pure sine wave inverter, safety certification and a real warranty. A unit that fails or overstates capacity is not a bargain at any price. Read recent owner reviews before buying."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do portable power stations lose charge when not in use?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, slowly. Most lose a small percentage per month in storage, and leaving the display or outlets switched on drains them faster. Manufacturers typically recommend topping up every three to six months. Charge to full before storm season and whenever a severe weather warning goes out. Turn off unused outputs before storing it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What can a 1,000Wh power station run?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A typical full-size refrigerator for roughly 8 to 14 hours, a CPAP without humidifier for several nights, a router and modem for well over a day, or phones dozens of times. It will not run a space heater or air conditioner for long. Runtime depends on each device's real draw. Check the actual watts on each label."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can a portable power station save money on electricity?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Not meaningfully. Charging from the grid and using the power later costs the same electricity plus conversion losses, and battery wear adds cost. Time-of-use savings are a few cents a day. A portable solar panel produces too little to make a real difference to a household bill. Buy one for resilience, not savings."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a solar generator worth it over a regular power station?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A solar generator is just a power station bundled with panels. It is worth it if you camp off-grid or face outages long enough that you need to recharge without grid power. For short outages near home, a power station alone is enough, and you can add panels later. Solar panels typically last far longer than the battery."
