        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            A portable power station is worth it when your needs fit inside the battery: short outages, a CPAP, a refrigerator for a day, internet for work, camping trips and homes where a generator is impractical or prohibited. It is silent, safe indoors, nearly maintenance-free and, with LiFePO4 cells rated for thousands of cycles, likely to last many years.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            It is not worth it for whole-home backup, heavy heating or cooling, saving money on electricity, or long outages without solar, where a generator or installed system gives far more energy per dollar. Make a list of what you actually need to run, size the unit to it, plan how you will recharge, and the answer for your household becomes obvious.
          </p>
        </div>

